forced is already among top? The above works anyway.\n\nWe should also consider that forced might be for second round? But the fixture shows forced list with one element, and the first round target is larger, so plausible it's for the first round.\n\nAlternative: maybe forced applies to the round that has a matching target? But no.\n\nWrite the code with proper indentation.\n\nNow produce JSON. We'll write the Python code as a string, escaping newlines. Ensure no extra spaces.\n\nWe'll output:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    round_scores = payload['round_scores']\\n    round_targets = payload['round_targets']\\n    forced = payload['forced']\\n    result = []\\n    for i, scores in enumerate(round_scores):\\n        target = round_targets[i]\\n        if i == 0 and forced:\\n            forced_idx = forced[0]\\n            others = [(scores[j], j) for j in range(len(scores)) if j != forced_idx]\\n            others.sort(reverse=True, key=lambda x: x[0])\\n            selected = [forced_idx] + [idx for _, idx in others[:target-1]]\\n        else:\\n            items = [(scores[j], j) for j in range(len(scores))]\\n            items.sort(reverse=True, key=lambda x: x[0])\\n            selected = [idx for _, idx in items[:target]]\\n        result.append(selected)\\n    return {\\\"selected\\\": result}\"}\n\nBut we need to ensure that the string is properly JSON-escaped: The backslashes in \\n are actually newline characters in the source string. In JSON, we need to represent newline as \\n (two characters: backslash and n). So when we write the JSON string, we need to double-escape? Actually, we are constructing a JSON object where the value of \"implementation\" is a string. That string must contain the actual Python source code, including newlines. In JSON, newline characters in strings are represented as \\n. So we need to replace each newline with \\n. Also, backslashes need to be escaped as \\\\, and double quotes